00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.160 align:start position:19%
And the people running it were the
greatest minds alive. Let me give you my

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:12.720 align:start position:19%
favorite. Eratosthenes. Around 240 BC,
he heard that in the city of Syene, at

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.280 align:start position:19%
noon on the summer solstice, the sun
shone straight down a well. No shadow at

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.320 align:start position:19%
all.
But in Alexandria, on the same day, a

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:25.480 align:start position:19%
stick did cast a shadow.
From that one difference, he calculated

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.360 align:start position:19%
the angle of the shadow, estimated the
distance between the two cities, and

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:37.160 align:start position:19%
computed the Earth's circumference.
About 39,000 km. The real number? Just

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:41.720 align:start position:19%
over 40,000. He was off by less than 2%
with a stick and a well.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:49.120 align:start position:19%
That to me is the Library of Alexandria
in one story. Curiosity turned into

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.120 align:start position:19%
mathematics, turned into truth about

